DATASOURCE works by: 
You’ll have two different Tempo YAML files with different jobs:
helm/tempo/values.yaml

This configures the Tempo application itself:
- replicas
- storage
- Workload Identity
- OpenTelemetry Protocol (OTLP) ports
- resources
- persistence
Then:
k8s/datasources/tempo.yaml

This configures Grafana to connect to Tempo as a datasource.
So:
helm/tempo/values.yaml
        ↓
deploys/configures Tempo

k8s/datasources/tempo.yaml
        ↓
tells Grafana where Tempo is
Loki is being used to store ingested logs from k8.
Grafana Alloy is an open-source, vendor-neutral telemetry collector and observability agent that leverages Loki for storage
Tempo monolithic is being used for storing and indexing traces from k8
Grafana ↔ Loki — datasource works; successfully queried real logs.
OpenTelemetry Collector is a vendor-agnostic proxy and service that receives, processes, and exports telemetry data like traces, metrics, and logs.

Grafana — the dashboard/UI.
Prometheus — collects and stores metrics.
Alertmanager — handles/routs alerts.
kube-state-metrics — exposes Kubernetes object state as metrics.
node-exporter — exposes node CPU, RAM, disk, network metrics.
Alloy — collects logs and sends them to Loki.
Loki — stores/queries logs.
Loki gateway/cache/canary — supporting Loki components.
Tempo — stores/queries traces.
OpenTelemetry Collector — will receive/process traces and send them to Tempo.
